  "id": "COMPOUND:24420 - PROTEIN:6075",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a complex network of interactions involving Npc151898 and the endoplasmic reticulum aminopeptidase 2 (ERAP2). The core mechanism appears to involve the targeting of ERAP2 through multiple pathways, with Npc151898 acting as a central node that influences the activity of the Werner syndrome ATP-dependent helicase. This helicase is targeted by Excisusin B and 19-Oxo-3-beta,5,14-trihydroxy-5-beta-bufa-20,22-dienolide-3-acetate, which in turn target ERAP2. These compounds may modulate ERAP2 activity through distinct mechanisms, potentially affecting protein degradation or processing in the endoplasmic reticulum. Additionally, Npc151898 is also linked to Calmodulin, which is targeted by Volvalerenal E, further suggesting a broader regulatory network. The integration of these pathways implies that Npc151898 may influence ERAP2 through a series of molecular interactions, possibly involving enzyme inhibition or regulatory modulation. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Npc151898 and ERAP2, though the exact nature of the interactions remains to be fully elucidated.",
